1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,225
Automate your Claude Code work in plain English, with ShellX Automations.

2
00:00:06,557 --> 00:00:12,207
Automations keeps your Claude Code skills, workflows and scheduled jobs in one place.

3
00:00:12,207 --> 00:00:16,860
Each card shows its schedule, the next run, and how the last run went.

4
00:00:18,207 --> 00:00:21,876
To make a new one, press New and describe what you want.

5
00:00:21,876 --> 00:00:26,594
Here: every weekday at nine, summarize the pull requests in my web repo.

6
00:00:33,795 --> 00:00:37,100
Claude Code turns that into a draft cronjob: a name,

7
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:42,565
a schedule, the folder it runs in, and the only commands it may use while you're away.

8
00:00:43,904 --> 00:00:49,087
It always asks how you want to hear back. I pick when it's done and when it fails, on my Mac.

9
00:00:56,363 --> 00:01:01,423
The draft is drawn as a flowchart, so you can check the logic before anything runs.

10
00:01:01,423 --> 00:01:04,959
The Text tab shows the skill file Claude Code will follow.

11
00:01:05,581 --> 00:01:09,057
Press Save, and ShellX schedules it on this Mac.

12
00:01:10,796 --> 00:01:14,357
The new card shows its next run. Run now tries it right away:

13
00:01:14,357 --> 00:01:17,859
the card turns yellow while it works, then shows the result.

14
00:01:20,905 --> 00:01:24,240
Skills you already have in Claude Code show up here too.

15
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:29,420
Open one, and Claude draws its flowchart, including the step where it stops to ask you.

16
00:01:30,951 --> 00:01:35,623
ShellX is free for Mac. Get it at shellx.pro.
